        # CALIBRATION, over-determined on BOTH axes:
        #   ordinate  nine printed labels 0.0..3.5 by 0.5, spacing 69.5/68.5/
        #             69.5/69.5/69.0/69.5/68.0 px -> D = (541.5 - y)/138.1428,
        #             which puts D = 0.0 exactly on the bottom frame (y 541)
        #             and D = 3.5 on the top frame (y 57);
        #   abscissa  a TOP axis of six labels -4.0..+1.0 in log10 exposure,
        #             spacing 77.0/75.5/74.0/75.5/74.5 px, spanning the frame
        #             exactly, AND a BOTTOM axis titled "Camera Stops" of
        #             seventeen labels -8..+8.
        # (Both axis titles and all 26 tick labels were read glyph by glyph off
        # the pixel grid; the chart title is "Log Exposure".)
        # WARNING THE TWO ABSCISSAE ON CINESTILL'S OWN CHART DISAGREE BY 3.7 %.
        # Sixteen stops are drawn across exactly 5.00 decades, i.e. 3.20
        # stops/decade, where a stop is 0.30103 decades and sixteen stops are
        # 4.82 decades. The LOG axis was taken as authoritative -- it is the
        # primary sensitometric abscissa, its label spacing is uniform to
        # +/-1 px, and it lands on the frame edges -- and the stops axis was
        # used only for its ZERO, which fixes metered mid-grey at log E
        # -1.51681. All 1440 traced points were then shifted by +1.51681 so
        # that mid-grey sits at x = 0, which is this database's convention
        # (see speed_point_x). The same 3.7 % inconsistency appears on
        # CineStill's second chart, so it is a property of their plotting and
        # not a misreading of one figure.
        # FIT: the six ToneCurve parameters were solved per layer by
        # coordinate descent on all 480 points. Residuals, in density:
        #   r  rms 0.0197, max 0.039     g  rms 0.0248, max 0.060
        #   b  rms 0.0154, max 0.053
