#: (derived merge relocated below _PARAM_SOURCES_DEVELOPER -- see there)

#: DEVELOPER / PROCESS IDENTITY MINED FROM THE ON-DISK SHEETS (task EM-A7,
#: 2026-08-27). Applied on top of `_PROCESSING` above; these entries carry a
#: developer or process NAME and nothing else, because a name is all the sheet
#: states -- no time, no temperature, no agitation, and inventing those would be
#: fabrication.
#:
#: WHY THIS MATTERS AND IS NOT BOOKKEEPING. Development progress type is a
#: property of the DEVELOPER, not of the emulsion: Tani gets type A out of CP-20
#: and type B out of D72 using the SAME emulsion (Figs. 7.8/7.9, traced in
#: EMULSION_KNOWLEDGE_BASE.md 23b). `DevelopmentProgress` therefore cannot be
#: set on a profile whose developer is unknown, which is why it reached only 9
#: stocks. This dict is the first step out of that.
#:
#: ⚠ HOW THE FALSE POSITIVES WERE REJECTED, BECAUSE THE NEXT PERSON WILL HIT
#: THEM TOO. A keyword sweep over the whole corpus proposed 29 candidates.
#: TWELVE WERE WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE LOOKED RIGHT: a general film-
#: restoration monograph on disk mentions "ECN-2" twice, and matching on it
#: assigned ECN-2 to AGFACOLOR NEU 1936, DUFAYCOLOR 1937, SOVIET PANCHROM 1939
#: and EASTMANCOLOR 5248 (1953) -- every one of them decades before ECN-2
#: existed (1974). Two more assigned Portra's C-41 sheet to the ULTRA COLOR
#: stocks, which are C-41 films but are not that document.
#: THE RULE APPLIED: a process is accepted ONLY from the stock's OWN datasheet.
#: A mention in a third document is not evidence about this film.
